A||||4||Tom88gt||replaceing coolant crossover tube?||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||05-11-2000||08:46 AM||||Has anyone replaced their rear coolant cross over tube?  I need to do mine soon.  I already have a stainless steel replacement.  I'm guessing I'll have to remove the CAT.  Are there any other surprises I should plan for.  I don't want to be in the middle of it and find I need to do something unexpected.||||reg||5
Z||000002||1FST2M6||05-11-2000||09:21 AM||||have new hoses to replace the ones on the car.. odds are you'll get in there and have to cut one of them off... other than that its REALLY easy... two bolts for the exhaust, some springs... a 13mm nut or 2 and a flat head screw driver... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tom88gt||05-11-2000||01:01 PM||||It sounds like you didn't have to mess with removing anything else, like the catalytic converter for example.||||reg||5
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||05-11-2000||01:21 PM||||Did this last year. Don't have to fool with the cat, but new hoses are a good idea. What will **stink** is that you'll need to drop the compressor and both of its mounts to get the pipe out. At least, this **stinks** if you plan to keep the A/C. Otherwise, your car is a bit lighter and has a bit less drag on the engine....||||reg||3
Z||000005||Raydar||05-11-2000||05:31 PM||||Tom, <BR>If your cat has a heat shield (mine didn't, but the cat was aftermarket) you may want to remove it. In addition to the top compressor bracket, I had to drop my starter in order to get enough "wiggle room". Remove the oil filter, too. <BR>When you put it all back together, don't forget the grounds, and be sure to keep the wiring harness away from the manifold.  <P>Have fun. I know I did. <P>Travis... Easy?!? Did you drop your engine to replace the pipe? This was about the biggest pain-in-the-ass thing that I've had to do to mine.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 05-11-2000).]||||reg||1
